   <dc:creator>Blanquart, Christophe</dc:creator>
   <dc:creator>Bertrand, Philippe</dc:creator>
   <dc:creator>Bolognesi, Maria Laura</dc:creator>
   <dc:subject>Cashew Nutshell Liquid</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Green Chemistry</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>One Health</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Antiparasitic Activity</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Trypanosoma brucei brucei</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Leishmania amazonensis</dc:subject>
   <dc:description>Produção científica do Laboratório de Biologia Celular.</dc:description>
   <dc:description>The authors gratefully acknowledge support from National Council of Technological and Scientific Development (CNPq #401864/2013-8 (PVE), #312709/2017-0 (DT-2), and #308486/2020-0 (DT-2), from RPT11F (Rede de Plataformas Tecnológicas da Fiocruz, IOC/Fiocruz), from The Carlos Chagas Filho Foundation for Research Support of the State of Rio de Janeiro (FAPERJ), CNPq, Oswaldo Cruz Foundation, PAEF/CNPq/Fiocruz, and CAPES, ChemAxon (https:// chemaxon.com) for kindly providing academic free license of Marvin suite. Maria de Nazaré Correia Soeiro is a research fellow from CNPq and CNE. This work was developed within the COST Action One Health drugs against parasitic vector-borne diseases in Europe and beyond (OneHealthdrugs) CA21111.</dc:description>
   <dc:description>The search for new drugs fulfilling One Health and Green Chemistry requirements is an urgent call. Here, for the first time, we envisaged developing SAHA analogues by starting from the cashew nutshell liquid (CNSL) agro-industrial waste and employing a metathesis approach. This sustainable combination (comprising principles #7 and #9) allowed a straightforward synthesis of compounds 13–20. All of them were found to not be toxic on HepG2, IMR-32, and L929 cell lines. Then, their potential against major human and animal vector-borne parasitic diseases (VBPDs) was assessed. Compound 13 emerged as a green hit against the trypomastigote forms of T. b. brucei. In silico studies showed that the T. b. brucei HDAC (TbDAC) catalytic pocket could be occupied with a similar binding mode by both SAHA and 13, providing a putative explanation for its antiparasitic mechanism of action (13, EC₅₀ = 0.7 ± 0.2 μM).</dc:description>
   <dc:date>2024-09-25T17:44:02Z</dc:date>
   <dc:date>2024-09-25T17:44:02Z</dc:date>
